options_covered:
Both, with one engine.

OPTION 1 ("rush hour for electrons") is the outer loop. A day-ahead PACE traffic report is built from CAISO OASIS (EDAM day-ahead LMP with its congestion component, the SLD_FCST load forecast, and the EDAM wind and solar forecast), EIA-930 and EIA PACE.xlsx CO2. It sets the boundary conditions for the stack:
- Pulse's state-of-charge trajectory;
- a reserve floor for RMP's unplanned events;
- an hourly ramp limit that tightens in rush hours;
- the hourly envelope for deferrable compute.

A Duke-style headroom calendar (184 curtailment hours a year at 0.5%, all June to August afternoons and evenings) tells the stack when the capacity crunch can come at all.

OPTION 2 ("compute follows the sun") is the inner, "when" half. Deferrable training moves into PACE's midday solar trough. On Oct 27 the day-ahead price ran $21.38 at 3pm against $55.78 at 6pm, and generated CO2 on Oct 25 ran 504 against 817 g/kWh. Non-deferrable work is power-capped using Emerald's real cap-versus-throughput curve, and during curtailment events compute is the last layer, used after Spin and Pulse.

The multi-region "where" half is cut on purpose. torus-intel.md says Torus does not route compute between regions. An optional PACE vs PACW/PGE/SP15 price strip is the first item on the cut list.

nucleus_fit:
Probably enterable for the Nucleus bounty too. Whether double entry is allowed is unconfirmed, so ask the organizers at kickoff.

Rubric mapping:
- Progress beyond concept (30%). A working app on live public market data, with:
  - a nightly no-key pipeline;
  - a unit-tested optimizer that re-plans live;
  - a backtest across every EDAM day since May 1, 2026;
  - a "What's real" table.
  That is more than a mock-up.
- Path to reality (25%).
  - It plugs into a live program (Wattsmart, where Torus is one of two approved commercial vendors; torus-cases-a.findings #6) through a named interface: Lasso's API as a grid signal.
  - It runs in all three Torus utility territories from one public feed (PACE, PACW, PGE).
  - It uses only free data and needs no keys or new hardware.
  - The first customer is obvious: Torus's own utility pitch.
- Hard tech depth (20%). This is the weakest score, since it is software. Lean on the physics shown plainly:
  - C-rates (Spin 10C and a 6-minute tank against 5-6 minute calls; Pulse 0.5C and a 2-hour tank);
  - round-trip loss and cycle wear (365 of 7,500 Pulse cycles a year);
  - an exact DP optimizer;
  - carbon accounting and why average differs from marginal;
  - real electricity-market data engineering (OASIS zips, GMT versus Mountain time).
- Utah impact (15%):
  - PACE's 10,173 MW Jul 20 peak hour (utah-grid.findings #4);
  - Operation Gigawatt, which Torus's RMP MOU was announced under (torus-intel.findings #4);
  - the first SB 132 data-center contract priced hourly at EDAM (utah-grid.findings #21);
  - 2,602 MW of data centers under construction (utah-grid.findings #22);
  - Creekstone Delta running Torus Load Smoothing;
  - the evening rush and its fossil share (utah-grid.findings #6, #10).
- Team and pitch (10%): a solo founder running four coding-agent harnesses in disjoint lanes, a tight 3-minute story, and a judge-driven demo.
